\section*{Limitations}

Our findings are bounded by:
(1)~\emph{Scope}: model scale $\leq$14B (5 architecture families), English text (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv), four decoding strategies; arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ is confirmed with five models across four architecture families but with reduced sample size ($n{=}2{,}200$ per model).
(2)~\emph{Protocol}: prompt-chain (isolates text-intrinsic degradation); retrain-chain yields $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (Pythia-1.4B LoRA; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}), reflecting additional model-adaptation artifacts beyond text-level information loss.
(3)~\emph{Threshold}: $\Kempirical$ ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$; 8/9 configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Mistral-7B: $\Kempirical{=}1$).
(4)~\emph{Marginal boundary}: Qwen-continuation's 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.002; cross-scorer validation drops this to $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}). LLaMA-3.1-8B represents the most marginal $\Kempirical{=}2$ assignment, with the 1v2 CI lower bound within 0.3 percentage points of $\theta$.
(5)~\emph{Feature set}: the 15D features yield a feature-set-specific lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher); neural probe $\Kempirical$ is model-dependent ($3$ for Qwen, $2$ for Pythia/OLMo).
(6)~\emph{Segment length}: $\Kempirical{=}2$ confirmed for 128 and 256 tokens (CI lower $> \theta$); 512 tokens yields point estimate $\Kempirical{=}2$ but CI lower marginally below $\theta$ ($0.5979$), indicating the boundary weakens at longer generation lengths.
(7)~\emph{Curation applicability}: the filtering evaluation is validated on two architectures (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct and Pythia-1.4B) with consistent strategy ordering (Appendix~\ref{sec:pythia_filtering}), though absolute improvement remains small and effect sizes diminish at lower contamination ratios (10--50\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}).
Additional limitations (cross-model scoring sensitivity, feature ranking variability at the 1v2 boundary, structural composition confound analysis) appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing, positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use publicly available corpora (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv abstracts) and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.
Code and generated corpora will be released upon acceptance to enable reproducibility.

\bibliography{references}

\input{appendix}
\input{appendix_extended}

\end{document}

\section{Application: \texorpdfstring{$\Kstar$}{K*}-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

We test whether $\Kempirical$ has predictive value for curation strategy selection, using a single-model setup (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct) as proof of concept.
$\Kempirical = 2$ predicts that binary curation should suffice, and that graduated performance should converge toward binary as the decay steepness increases.
We compare six strategies on 30{,}000 documents (depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter}, (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from 15D LightGBM), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson}~\citep{drayson2025}, (iv)~\textbf{graduated} ($w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain above median), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir}.
We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs on MMLU (a multitask language understanding benchmark) and HellaSwag (a commonsense reasoning benchmark) across 5 seeds (51 total runs: 5 base strategies $\times$ 5 seeds $+$ 5 graduated-$\alpha$ values $\times$ 5 seeds $+$ 1 base-model reference; this 100\% synthetic setting is an upper bound~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, dohmatob2024model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Downstream task performance (MMLU, HellaSwag) under six data-filtering strategies. Binary-ours uses the 15D LightGBM depth classifier; Graduated applies exponentially decaying weights $w = \exp(-\alpha \hat{d})$. Mean $\pm$ std over 5 seeds. Full $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.}
\label{tab:tab_filtering_main}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}}
\end{table}

Binary-ours minimizes MMLU degradation most effectively (Table~\ref{tab:tab_filtering_main}), reducing the gap from $-$1.53\,pp (no-filter) to $-$0.77\,pp (${\sim}$50\% reduction; $p_{\text{corr}} < 0.05$ vs.\ all alternatives).
These effects are measured in a 100\% synthetic upper-bound setting; real-world contamination ratios are typically lower, and effect sizes would scale accordingly.
The strategy ordering is interpretable through $\Kempirical$: binary filtering exploits the one reliable boundary (0v1), while graduated strategies assign weights to depths 2--5 based on near-random classifier outputs, injecting noise.
Graduated filtering converges monotonically toward binary: at $\alpha{=}0.9$, it matches binary-ours on HellaSwag exactly ($\Delta = 0$, $p_{\text{corr}} = 1.00$) but remains significantly worse on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$).
DSIR is indistinguishable from no-filter, confirming that depth-aware features are necessary for curation in recursively contaminated corpora (Figure~\ref{fig:filtering_strategies} in Appendix).
Binary filtering retains a consistent advantage across contamination levels (10--50\%), with effect sizes diminishing 3--6$\times$ relative to the 100\% setting; graduated weighting remains indistinguishable from no-filter at all tested ratios, confirming that depth assignments beyond $\Kempirical$ inject noise regardless of severity (Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}).
Cross-model replication on Pythia-1.4B confirms the binary $>$ graduated ordering (Appendix~\ref{sec:pythia_filtering}), consistent with $\Kempirical{=}2$ predicting binary filtering as optimal granularity.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 8 C4 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{\geq}4$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$.
A single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from multiply recycled content, limiting depth-aware curation to a binary boundary.
Converging evidence supports a data-intrinsic interpretation: OBD achieves 96\% depth-0 recall but only 14--22\% for depths 2--4; frontier LLMs show 8--38\% (McNemar $p > 0.05$ vs.\ OBD; Table~\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}).

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
Token-level Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) between adjacent generations reveals two distinct decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff (0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$, median ${\sim}$15$\times$) followed by rapid convergence to a noise floor, while arXiv decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$).
Cross-model consistency indicates that $G_\delta$ contraction rate is domain-governed; the retrain-chain profile ($\lambda = 0.285$, no sharp cliff) reflects model-adaptation artifacts rather than text-intrinsic loss (Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).

\paragraph{Graduated filtering failure.}
For web text, 2v3 $\auc < \theta$ across all model configurations, so depth assignments for $d \geq 2$ approximate random labels; graduated weighting injects noise, explaining convergence toward binary at high $\alpha$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
The strategy ordering binary $>$ graduated aligns with the $\Kempirical{=}2$ prediction: graduated approaches cannot exploit depth information that does not exist beyond the discrimination boundary.
DSIR fails differently: bigram hashing captures domain shift but not depth-correlated signatures (pattern persists across 10--50\% contamination ratios; Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}).

\paragraph{Representation and scorer dependence.}
$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent: frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$, while Pythia and OLMo match the 15D boundary at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}); the 15D features trade one depth level for cross-model consistency (8/9 configurations $\Kempirical{\leq}2$).
$\Kempirical$ can also depend on scorer family: on Wikipedia, cross-family scoring reduces $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1 ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}), while on C4 it preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$) and $\Kempirical$ for 6/8 configurations (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).
Scorer sensitivity is inversely related to domain structural complexity; we recommend cross-family validation for new domains.

\paragraph{Domain dependence of $\Kempirical$.}
C4 web text yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (8/9 configurations, 1B--14B), while arXiv yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ across five models and four architecture families (Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}).
With scorer confound controlled, all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}), producing a uniform ordering governed by source-domain structural complexity: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).
The sole C4 exception (Mistral-7B, $\Kempirical{=}1$) has $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv, reinforcing the domain-governed interpretation (Appendix~\ref{sec:boundary_extended}).

\paragraph{Prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain $\Kempirical$.}
Retrain-chain experiments (Pythia-1.4B LoRA) yield $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (Table~\ref{tab:retrain_ci}), far exceeding prompt-chain $\Kempirical{=}2$; the former reflects model-adaptation artifacts rather than text-level information loss (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).



\paragraph{Why $\Kempirical$ matters.}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ for web text is not a negative result but a precise measurement: it rules out both $\Kempirical{=}1$ (no depth signal beyond 0v1) and $\Kempirical{\geq}3$ (graduated tracking feasible), while arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ confirms the framework captures real domain-level variation rather than a methodological ceiling.
$\Kempirical$ thus serves as a diagnostic of domain structural complexity and is directly prescriptive for curation granularity.



\paragraph{Practical implications.}
For web text ($\Kempirical{=}2$), graduated depth weighting injects noise beyond the boundary, validating binary filtering as optimal (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}); for arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$), an additional depth level carries discriminative signal.
Mixed-domain corpora thus require domain-stratified filtering, and $\Kempirical{=}2$ provides post-hoc grounding for the binary approach of \citet{drayson2025} (segment-length sensitivity in Table~\ref{tab:s11_length_ablation}).

